 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
 *   **Memory Limits:** Even for highly sparse volumes with $\alpha = 0.01$, the standard NeRF cache size is nearly 60 TB, which exceeds the memory capacity of consumer-grade hardware [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Sparsity Assumption:** The formulas rely on $\alpha$ to scale the position-dependent term. If $\alpha$ is not accurately estimated, the memory prediction will be incorrect.
-*   **Data Type Sensitivity:** The calculation is highly sensitive to the bit sizes $s_{rgb}, s_\sigma, s_{uvw}, s_{\beta}$. Using single-precision floats instead of half-precision would double the memory usage for those components [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The calculation is $O(1)$ with respect to scene content, depending only on the grid dimensions ($k, l$) and data type sizes.
-*   **Utility:** Used during architecture selection to determine if a target resolution ($k, l$) is feasible on available hardware [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitation:** Does not account for overhead from the sparse spatial structure (e.g., voxel grid headers) or runtime memory for ray traversal buffers [S1, unofficial].
